🧠 What Is Gap Analysis?
Gap analysis is a structured process used to evaluate how an organization’s current cybersecurity posture compares to the standards and recommendations of a chosen framework (e.g., NIST CSF, ISO 27001). It identifies missing, incomplete, or misconfigured security controls and helps prioritize improvements.

🧩 Why It Matters
• 	Clarifies current capabilities: Helps organizations objectively assess their cybersecurity strengths and weaknesses.
• 	Guides investment: Prioritizes resources toward the most critical gaps.
• 	Supports compliance: Provides documentation for regulatory audits and legal requirements.
• 	Improves resilience: Strengthens defenses against evolving threats.

🛠️ How It Works
1. Framework Alignment
Each function in the cybersecurity framework (Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover) includes specific goals or outcomes. For example:
• 	Identify: Maintain an inventory of IT assets.
• 	Protect: Implement access controls and encryption.
• 	Detect: Monitor systems for anomalies.
• 	Respond: Develop incident response plans.
• 	Recover: Establish data backup and restoration procedures.
Each outcome is supported by security controls—technical, administrative, or physical measures.

2. Gap Identification
The analysis compares:
• 	Current controls in place
• 	Recommended controls from the framework
It highlights:
• 	Controls that are missing
• 	Controls that are poorly configured
• 	Controls that are outdated or ineffective

3. Scoring and Reporting
A typical gap analysis report includes: | Function | Missing Controls | Risk Impact (CIA) | Remediation Target | |---------|------------------|-------------------|--------------------| | Protect | 5 of 12 missing | High risk to confidentiality | Q2 2026 | | Detect | 3 poorly configured | Moderate risk to integrity | Q1 2026 |
• 	CIA refers to Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability
• 	Remediation Target sets deadlines for fixing gaps

4. Remediation Planning
The report provides:
• 	Recommendations for each gap
• 	Prioritization based on risk level
• 	Action plans for implementation

👥 Who Performs It?
• 	Internal Security Teams: May conduct initial assessments.
• 	Third-Party Consultants: Often brought in for expertise, objectivity, and regulatory insight.
External specialists help:
• 	Interpret complex frameworks
• 	Spot overlooked vulnerabilities
• 	Stay updated on best practices

🔁 When to Conduct Gap Analysis
• 	Initial framework adoption
• 	New compliance requirements
• 	Periodic reviews (e.g., every 2–3 years)
• 	Post-incident assessments

